<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Community Is Famous For... – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-style: italic; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Community News</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Our Community Is Famous For...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Respond to questions about requesting information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize the main ideas in school news reports about community events (e.g., 'The school is organizing a clean-up day in the park next Saturday.' 'The class will visit Casco Viejo to learn about Panama's history.').
                Can respond to simple questions when classmates ask for information about community events (e.g., 'Where is the school fair?' 'It's at the sports field.' 'When are the community's carnivals?' 'They are next year starting on February 14.' 'Where is the Fiestas Patrias parade?' 'It's on Central Avenue.').
                Can identify specific details in conversations about local traditions and places (e.g., 'Our community is famous for its traditional dances.' 'In Panama, people celebrate the Desfile de las Mil Polleras in Las Tablas with colorful dresses and parades.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of famous community events and landmarks in Panama (e.g., Desfile de las Mil Polleras, Casco Viejo, local festivals, a colorful Pollera, a sombrero Pintao). Students look at the pictures. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and "Is your community famous for any of these?" The teacher introduces key vocabulary: <i>community</i>, <i>festival</i>, <i>parade</i>, <i>artisan</i>, <i>Pollera</i>, <i>sombrero Pintao</i>, <i>colorful</i>, <i>traditional</i>. Students echo the words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models pronunciation of the new vocabulary using flashcards and realia where possible. The teacher also introduces and models the pronunciation of past simple verbs related to community events: 'visited', 'celebrated', 'liked', 'prepared'. The teacher explicitly models the -ed endings (/t/, /d/, /ɪd/) by saying words like 'liked' /laɪkt/, 'moved' /muːvd/, 'wanted' /wɒntɪd/ and having students repeat. The teacher says, "Last year, I <i>visited</i> the Flowers Festival. It was very <i>colorful</i>."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary and grammar.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'festival' a daily event or a special event?" (Expected answer: "A special event.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'colorful' mean it has many colors or one color?" (Expected answer: "Many colors.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'I visited,' did I go yesterday or tomorrow?" (Expected answer: "Yesterday/in the past.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Community News".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a short (approx. 1.5 minutes) school news report and a brief interview segment about a recent community fair or cultural event in a Panamanian community. The report integrates target vocabulary (e.g., <i>community fair</i>, <i>reporter</i>, <i>news</i>, <i>mural</i>, <i>market</i>, <i>banner</i>, <i>celebrate</i>, <i>describe</i>) and grammar (past simple tense, adjectives for physical appearance like <i>tall</i>, <i>slender</i>, <i>curly hair</i>, <i>black eyes</i>, and compound sentences with 'but' e.g., "I liked the food, but I didn't like the crowd.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can recognize the main ideas in school news reports about community events and identify specific details in conversations about local traditions and places.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen for the main idea. Teacher asks: "What is the news report about?" "Where did this event happen?" (e.g., "It's about the San Miguel community fair." "It happened at the school sports field.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Listening:</b> The teacher plays the audio a second time. Students listen for specific details and answer True/False questions on a worksheet.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The community fair was last weekend." (True/False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The reporter saw a tall artisan with black straight hair." (True/False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The people interviewed liked the loud music at the fair." (True/False)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific information (who, what, where, when), identifying past simple verbs, recognizing adjectives for physical appearance, understanding compound sentences with 'but'. Practice pronunciation of past tense -ed endings (/t/, /d/, /ɪd/) and identifying short and long vowel sounds (e.g., /æ/ in 'banner' vs. /eɪ/ in 'parade').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening Gap Fill):</b> The teacher provides a partial transcript of the news report. Students listen to specific sections of the audio again (focused listening) and fill in the missing past simple verbs or target vocabulary words (e.g., "The students _______ a colorful mural." "The _______ was very friendly."). This reinforces listening for specific words and past tense recognition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and Match/Identify):</b> The teacher plays short sentences or descriptions from the audio.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>a) Identify Pronunciation:</b> Students listen to a word with a past tense -ed ending and raise their hand if they hear /t/, /d/, or /ɪd/. (e.g., 'liked' /t/, 'celebrated' /ɪd/, 'described' /d/).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>b) Physical Description Match:</b> The teacher reads descriptions of people from the audio (e.g., "She was tall and had curly hair." "He was short and wore a sombrero Pintao."). Students match the description to a picture or name from the news report characters.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>c) Vowel Sound Recognition:</b> The teacher says pairs of words from the vocabulary list (e.g., 'banner' and 'parade', 'tradition' and 'queen'). Students listen and identify if the vowel sounds are short or long, reinforcing phonemic awareness.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Confirm):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays short statements from the audio. One student listens and says if the statement is correct or incorrect based on what they heard. The other student confirms or corrects. (e.g., Teacher: "The reporter *didn't like* the colorful floats." Student A: "Incorrect! The reporter *liked* the colorful floats, but didn't like the loud music."). This activity allows students to practice expressing preferences heard and softening disagreement ("I like parades, too, but I prefer to attend music shows during the festival.").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Community News".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Guided Listening & Information Gathering.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher plays a series of short audio clips (e.g., 3-4 different 30-second "news flashes" or "interviews") about various community events or traditions from different Panamanian regions (e.g., a festival, a parade, a market). Each clip contains details about who, what, where, when, physical descriptions, and a preference statement (using 'but').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Each group has a "Community Event Fact Sheet." For each audio clip, students listen carefully and collaboratively fill in the fact sheet, noting:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Event Name:</b> (e.g., "Chitré's Carnival," "Portobelo's Festival")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Where:</b> (e.g., "on Central Avenue," "at the sports field")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>When:</b> (e.g., "last February," "next Saturday")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Who was described?</b> (e.g., "Carnival Queen," "artisan," "reporter")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Physical description heard:</b> (e.g., "tall and slender," "curly hair and black eyes")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>What was liked/disliked?</b> (e.g., "liked the colorful dresses, but didn't like the heat")</li>
                </ul>
                After listening to all clips and completing their sheets, each group shares one interesting fact they gathered from the audio with the class. This encourages exchanging personal information about their community and sharing past experiences/preferences (heard from the audio) and describing community members.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist & Short Listening Quiz.</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during Stage 4 to note students' ability to work collaboratively and extract information from the audio. For individual assessment, students complete a short listening comprehension quiz based on one of the audio clips from Stage 4 (or a new, very short clip).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students answer 4-5 multiple-choice and short-answer questions about the chosen audio clip, focusing on identifying:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Main idea of the report.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Specific details (who, where, when).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A physical description of a person mentioned.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An opinion or preference expressed by a speaker (e.g., "The speaker liked X, but didn't like Y.").</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying main ideas and specific details from the audio (Listening comprehension).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of past simple tense verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify physical descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in understanding compound sentences with 'but'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation in group listening tasks (observed).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned new words like _______ and _______. They helped me understand the news reports."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listening to the news reports was _______ (easy/difficult) because _______."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting community event or tradition you heard about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can listening carefully to news reports help you learn about your community and others?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? Did students actively engage with the audio materials and respond to questions about requesting information?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face with specific vocabulary, grammar, or pronunciation? What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for subsequent listening activities?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the audio materials at the appropriate CEFR A2.1 level for 6th graders?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 2 - Reading), we will read a written news report or article about a community festival or event, using many of the same vocabulary words and ideas we heard today. We will focus on understanding the text and identifying specific information from written content."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>